From b9cec91e758faa772e2a68e57978ae5f1d8c4f5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 09:30:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: scan a script the same command
 writes before it runs it

`cat > F <<'EOF' ... EOF; bash F` ran F's heredoc body, but the referenced-script walk reads F
from disk at scan time: missing (nothing to scan) or stale (old content). The guard allowed it
while real Bash executed the payload (t_baf36593e158398d, found on PR #2929's baseline).

Policy: both options from the card, combined conservatively.
- Every file the walked command writes with an output redirection (`>`, `>>`, `>|`, `&>`,
  `>&F`) or `tee` is recorded with what it writes, and that text is walked wherever the
  command runs the path (`bash F`, `sh F`, `. F`, `source F`, `./F`, stdin feeds), in the
  candidate's own mode, alongside the on-disk text (an append keeps it).
- Known content: `cat`/`tee` fed only by a heredoc or here-string (a quoted body verbatim; an
  unquoted body holding `$(`, backticks or process substitution is unknown), and `echo`/`printf`
  with literal arguments (escapes decoded, via the existing printed-line helpers).
- Any other writer (`rev`, `base64 -d`, `curl`, `python3 gen.py`, `cat a b`, stderr, `3<> F`)
  is unknown: executing that path fails closed; mentioning it does not, and a Python program
  still never fails closed. `<> F` on stdin (`bash <> F`) is input, not a write.
- Matching is by resolved path, with the same lexical model as the rest of the walk (no order,
  control-flow or `cd` tracking). Top-level fail-closed behaviour and all shell-fed and
  file-mediated detection are unchanged; the new module is cron/lifecycle_guard_generated.py.

RED/GREEN: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_generated_scripts.py runs each command in real Bash
after scanning it (F absent, or stale) and asserts the marker ran and the guard refused. On
origin/main 22 of 34 fail; with the fix all pass (36 with the two added allow cases).
